  {
   "id": "what",
   "h2": "What an adjective does",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "lead": "An adjective describes a noun: \"a happy slave\", \"a big house\". In Latin it changes its ending to match the noun it describes. This is called <b>agreement</b>."
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "<u class=\"c\">servus</u> <u class=\"v\">laetus</u> laborat.",
      "en": "<b>The happy slave</b> is working.",
      "note": "<em>laetus</em> is masculine, singular and nominative, like <em>servus</em>."
     }
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "domina <u class=\"c\">villam</u> <u class=\"v\">magnam</u> habet.",
      "en": "The mistress has <b>a big house</b>.",
      "note": "<em>magnam</em> is feminine, singular and accusative, like <em>villam</em>."
     }
    },
    {
     "facts": [
      "An adjective agrees with its noun in <b>gender</b>, <b>number</b> and <b>case</b>.",
      "In Latin the adjective usually comes after its noun. In English it goes before: <em>servus laetus</em>, \"the happy slave\".",
      "Adjectives of size and number often come first: <em>multi servi</em>, \"many slaves\"."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
