  {
   "id": "agree",
   "h2": "Matching, with different endings",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "p": "Agreement is about gender, number and case. The endings often look alike, but they need not. <em>nauta</em>, sailor, is 1st declension but masculine, so the adjective that goes with it is masculine."
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "<u class=\"c\">nauta</u> <u class=\"v\">laetus</u> navigat.",
      "en": "<b>The happy sailor</b> is sailing.",
      "note": "<em>nauta</em> ends in <em>-a</em>, but a sailor is a man, so <em>laetus</em> is masculine."
     }
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "servus <u class=\"c\">vinum</u> <u class=\"v\">bonum</u> portat.",
      "en": "The slave carries <b>good wine</b>.",
      "note": "<em>vinum</em> is neuter, so <em>bonum</em> is neuter: accusative singular."
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "translate",
   "h2": "Translating them",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "facts": [
      "Find the noun, then the adjective that matches it in gender, number and case.",
      "Put the English adjective before the noun: \"the angry master\".",
      "An adjective after <em>est</em> or <em>erat</em> describes the subject: <em>dominus iratus erat</em>, \"the master was angry\".",
      "A plural adjective on its own can stand for people: <em>multi</em>, \"many people\"; <em>Romani</em>, \"the Romans\"."
     ]
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "<u class=\"c\">dominus</u> <u class=\"v\">iratus</u> erat.",
      "en": "<b>The master</b> was <b>angry</b>."
     }
    },
    {
     "example": {
      "la": "servus <u class=\"c\">dominum</u> <u class=\"v\">iratum</u> timebat.",
      "en": "The slave was afraid of <b>the angry master</b>.",
      "note": "<em>iratum</em> is accusative, so it goes with <em>dominum</em>: the master is the angry one."
     }
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "watch",
   "h2": "Watch out",
   "exam": true,
   "label": "Common mistakes",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "facts": [
      "An adjective can have a different ending from its noun and still agree: <em>nauta laetus</em>.",
      "<em>-a</em> on an adjective can be feminine singular or neuter plural: <em>multa verba</em>, \"many words\".",
      "With two nouns, match the adjective by its case: in <em>servus dominum iratum timebat</em>, the master is the angry one.",
      "English puts the adjective first: <em>puella pulchra</em> is \"the beautiful girl\"."
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
